  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is running hot and pulling the company forward. Yesterday's 110% revenue jump was driven by 5 active groomers (vs. 2 last Monday) producing $526 per groomer\u2014a 4% lift above trailing baseline\u2014coupled with a 124% transaction spike. That's real capacity gain translating to real output. The add-on attach rate dipped from 82% to 57%, which suggests a small upsell discipline gap worth coaching, but it's noise relative to the headline: you're staffed stronger and the floor is executing. MTD Port is tracking 1.8% below DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014calendar-neutral, flat performance, not a miss. YTD you're up 52% revenue and 53% appointments versus last year. That's the story.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday one groomer generated $245\u201466% below the trailing-30-day baseline of $371/groomer/day. Retail cratered 58% while core grooms held flat, signaling front-of-house focus is fractured. More troubling: visit-cadence drift is real. The 12+ week visit-gap bucket grew 1.8 percentage points (now 10.2%), a leading indicator of customer stretch and early churn. MTD Hicksville is down 19% versus May, and while the DOW adjustment shows you're 3.2% ahead of calendar expectation, that's a thin margin given the lapse pool of 58 customers worth $40K annually is now actively drifting. You need to reverse that cadence trend immediately\u2014phone calls, reactivation outreach, and a hard look at why retail isn't moving.\n\nPattern: both locations have bather-hour volatility (Port yesterday 6h vs. 4.9h baseline; Hicksville consistently zero). Bather hours drive groomer throughput and margin. Schedule consistency matters.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port has 6 groomers and 0 bather hours today, 6 groomers and 7 bather hours tomorrow; Hicksville has 2 and 2 respectively; next 7 days Port is 29 groomer-days and 43 bather hours, Hicksville is 11 groomer-days and zero bather hours.**"
